<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Harness.RigControl</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>rig-control</AssemblyName>

    <!--
      Same open question, same answer, as Harness.S7's project file: Sharp7 is not committed to this
      repository, and the path is an overridable property defaulting to the machine-local copy. Do not
      change it here without changing it there — two projects resolving the same DLL differently is a
      way to build one half against a version the other has never seen.
    -->
    <Sharp7Path Condition="'$(Sharp7Path)' == ''">$(USERPROFILE)\.ladder\lib\Sharp7.dll</Sharp7Path>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THIS BUILD CAN PUT A CPU INTO RUN, AND THE PROJECT FILE IS THE FIRST PLACE THAT IS VISIBLE.

    It is the opposite claim to Harness.RigWrite's, and it is stated here for the same reason: the
    reference list is what a reader checks first.

      - DeviceGuard comes in through Harness.S7, and is the fence. There is no path to a device in
        this assembly that does not pass RunTransitionFence first (RigControlCli, section 1).
      - Harness.S7 is referenced for S7Status and S7RunStateReading — the run-state DECODER
        specifically, which carries a measured fact about this CPU and a regression guard. Reusing it
        is strictly better than a second copy that would drift.
      - Sharp7 is referenced DIRECTLY, because IS7Client deliberately does not expose PlcHotStart and
        must not start doing so. See IRunTransitionTransport for why the verb lives on its own
        interface in its own assembly rather than widening that one.

    What this assembly must NOT be able to reach, checked by an IL walk rather than asserted here:
    PlcStop, PlcColdStart, DBWrite, MBWrite, WriteArea, Download, Delete, PlcCopyRamToRom.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.S7\Harness.S7.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="Exists('$(Sharp7Path)')">
    <Reference Include="Sharp7">
      <HintPath>$(Sharp7Path)</HintPath>
      <Private>true</Private>
    </Reference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="Exists('$(Sharp7Path)')">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);SHARP7</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Without Sharp7 the live transport is excluded and the composition root hands the CLI a factory
    that throws with a named reason. The fence, the CLI, every gate and every exit code still build
    and still test — which is the point: the safety-relevant half of this tool has no dependency on
    being able to reach a device.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="!Exists('$(Sharp7Path)')">
    <Compile Remove="Sharp7RunTransition.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <Target Name="WarnMissingSharp7" BeforeTargets="Build" Condition="!Exists('$(Sharp7Path)')">
    <Message Importance="high"
             Text="Harness.RigControl: Sharp7 not found at '$(Sharp7Path)' — this build has NO device transport. The fence and the CLI still build and test. Override with -p:Sharp7Path=&lt;path&gt;." />
  </Target>

</Project>
